UPDATE module_contents SET learn_html = '<div class="content-card" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f0f4ff,#e8f0fe);border:1px solid #c3d9f7;"><h3 style="color:#1a56db;">📚 Research Connection</h3><p style="font-size:0.92rem;color:#374151;line-height:1.75;">This module aligns with the SAMR Model <a href="https://hippasus.com/resources/tte/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#3b306b;text-decoration:underline;" data-citation="true">(Puentedura, 2006)</a>, which describes four levels of technology integration: Substitution, Augmentation, Modification, and Redefinition. When educators first explore AI tools like ChatGPT, they typically begin at the Substitution level — using AI to do what they could do without it. The goal of this foundational module is to help educators recognize opportunities to move toward Modification and Redefinition, where AI enables fundamentally new learning experiences.</p><p style="font-size:0.92rem;color:#374151;line-height:1.75;margin-top:0.75rem;">
<strong>Additional Research Foundations:</strong> Beyond the SAMR Model, this module draws on Mishra &amp; Koehler''s (2006) <em>TPACK Framework</em> (Technological Pedagogical Content Knowledge), which argues that effective technology integration requires the intersection of technological knowledge, pedagogical knowledge, and content knowledge. AI tools amplify this intersection by enabling teachers to rapidly prototype instructional approaches that blend all three domains. Furthermore, Ertmer &amp; Ottenbreit-Leftwich (2010) found that teachers'' beliefs about technology are the strongest predictor of classroom integration — making this foundational exploration of AI capabilities essential for shifting mindsets from skepticism to informed adoption.</p>
<p style="font-size:0.92rem;color:#374151;line-height:1.75;margin-top:0.5rem;">
Luckin et al.''s (2016) <em>Intelligence Unleashed</em> report identifies three key roles AI can play in education: intelligent tutoring systems, AI-powered learning analytics, and AI as a tool for teacher professional development. Understanding these categories helps educators identify where AI can add the most value in their specific context.</p><p></p></div><div class="content-card" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#fef3c7,#fde68a);border:1px solid #f59e0b;margin-top:1rem;">
<h3 style="color:#92400e;">🧠 Meta-Learning Moment</h3>
<p style="font-size:0.92rem;color:#374151;line-height:1.75;"><strong>Notice what''s happening right now:</strong> As you explore this module, you''re experiencing the same learning process your students will go through when encountering AI for the first time. Pay attention to your own reactions — curiosity, skepticism, excitement, anxiety. These are the same emotions your students will feel. This awareness is called <em>metacognition</em> <a href="https://psycnet.apa.org/record/1980-09388-001" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#3b306b;text-decoration:underline;" data-citation="true">(Flavell, 1979)</a> — thinking about your own thinking. Research by Schraw &amp; Dennison (1994) shows that metacognitive awareness significantly improves learning outcomes. By modeling this reflective practice with AI, you''re not just learning a tool — you''re developing a pedagogical stance that values transparency, critical thinking, and intellectual humility.</p>
<p style="font-size:0.88rem;color:#6b7280;margin-top:0.5rem;font-style:italic;">💡 <strong>Try this:</strong> Before moving to the Practice phase, write down three assumptions you currently hold about AI in education. After completing the simulations, revisit these assumptions. What changed? This is the same reflective cycle you can use with students.</p></div><div class="content-card" style="margin-top:1rem;">
<h3>🔑 Key Concepts &amp; Vocabulary</h3>
<div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:0.5rem;">
<div style="padding:0.5rem;background:#f9fafb;border-radius:0.5rem;"><strong>Generative AI</strong><br><span style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#6b7280;">AI systems that create new content (text, images, code) based on patterns learned from training data</span></div>
<div style="padding:0.5rem;background:#f9fafb;border-radius:0.5rem;"><strong>Prompt Engineering</strong><br><span style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#6b7280;">The skill of crafting effective instructions for AI to produce useful, accurate outputs</span></div>
<div style="padding:0.5rem;background:#f9fafb;border-radius:0.5rem;"><strong>Hallucination</strong><br><span style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#6b7280;">When AI generates plausible-sounding but factually incorrect information</span></div>
<div style="padding:0.5rem;background:#f9fafb;border-radius:0.5rem;"><strong>SAMR Model</strong><br><span style="font-size:0.85rem;color:#6b7280;">Framework for evaluating technology integration depth: Substitution, Augmentation, Modification, Redefinition <a href="https://hippasus.com/resources/tte/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#3b306b;text-decoration:underline;" data-citation="true">(Puentedura, 2006)</a></span></div>
</div></div><div class="content-card" style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f0fdf4,#dcfce7);border:1px solid #86efac;margin-top:1rem;">
<h3 style="color:#166534;">🤔 Pre-Practice Reflection</h3>
<ol style="font-size:0.92rem;color:#374151;line-height:1.85;padding-left:1.25rem;">
<li>What is your current comfort level with AI tools on a scale of 1-5? What would move you one level higher?</li>
<li>How might AI change the way you approach your most time-consuming professional task?</li>
<li>What concerns do you have about AI in education, and how might you investigate whether those concerns are warranted?</li>
</ol></div><div class="content-card"><h3>Core Strategies</h3><div class="role-content" data-role="classroom" style="display: block;"><div class="strategy-section"><h4>Strategies for Teacher</h4><ul class="strategy-list"><li>Co-create AI usage guidelines with students to foster a culture of academic integrity and responsible use.</li><li>Model how to critically evaluate AI-generated content by fact-checking and comparing information from multiple sources.</li><li>Use an AI detector to spot-check student work, but use it as a conversation starter rather than a punitive tool.</li></ul></div></div><div class="role-content" data-role="leader" style="display: none;"><div class="strategy-section"><h4>Strategies for School/District Leader</h4><ul class="strategy-list"><li>Develop a district-wide acceptable use policy for generative AI that provides clear guidance for teachers, students, and families.</li><li>Provide professional development opportunities for staff to build their AI literacy and pedagogical skills.</li><li>Invest in secure, education-specific AI platforms to protect student data and ensure compliance with privacy laws.</li></ul></div></div><div class="role-content" data-role="sped" style="display: none;"><div class="strategy-section"><h4>Strategies for Special Education</h4><ul class="strategy-list"><li>Use AI to generate differentiated reading passages or scaffolded writing prompts for students with diverse learning needs.</li><li>Explore AI-powered assistive technologies that can support students with communication or executive functioning challenges.</li><li>Critically evaluate AI tool outputs to ensure they are appropriate and effective for individual student goals and IEPs.</li></ul></div></div><div class="role-content" data-role="support" style="display: none;"><div class="strategy-section"><h4>Strategies for Support Staff</h4><ul class="strategy-list"><li>Use AI to quickly draft parent communications or school-wide announcements, then personalize them before sending.</li><li>Translate school documents into multiple languages using AI to better communicate with multilingual families.</li><li>Train front office staff on the school''s AI policy to answer parent and community questions accurately.</li></ul></div></div><div class="role-content" data-role="coach" style="display: none;"><div class="strategy-section"><h4>Strategies for Instructional Coach</h4><ul class="strategy-list"><li>Curate a collection of high-quality AI prompts and resources aligned with the school''s instructional priorities.</li><li>Facilitate collaborative team discussions where teachers can share their experiences and problem-solve challenges related to AI in the classroom.</li><li>Model how to use AI as a thought partner for lesson planning, instructional design, and assessment creation.</li></ul></div></div><div class="role-content" data-role="higher_ed" style="display: none;"><div class="strategy-section"><h4>Strategies for Higher Ed Faculty</h4><ul class="strategy-list"><li>Incorporate AI literacy and ethics into teacher preparation programs to ensure new educators are ready for the AI-infused classroom.</li><li>Research the impact of AI on teaching and learning to inform evidence-based practices and policies.</li><li>Partner with K-12 districts to provide professional learning and support for practicing teachers on the effective use of AI.</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="safety-bias-grid"><div class="safety-box"><h4>Safety Checks</h4><ul><li>Always verify AI-generated content before sharing with students</li><li>Review outputs for age-appropriateness</li><li>Protect student data and privacy</li></ul></div><div class="bias-box"><h4>Bias Checks</h4><ul><li>Check for cultural, gender, and socioeconomic bias in AI outputs</li><li>Use diverse examples and perspectives</li><li>Teach students to recognize AI limitations</li></ul></div></div>
